{"reply":"Yes—the connection is worth examining, with one small correction to the scene you remembered. In Chapter 8, the gardeners explain that “this here ought to have been a _red_ rose-tree, and we put a white one in by mistake,” and they paint the roses before the Queen arrives (Chapter 8, *The Queen’s Croquet-Ground*). But the Queen does discover it: after examining the roses, she orders, “Off with their heads!” (Chapter 8, *The Queen’s Croquet-Ground*). Concealment is an anxious attempt to control the consequences, not a successful escape from them.

Your note describes a less extreme but structurally similar split: you fixed the billing mistake over the weekend and said nothing on Monday. A 2023 study of hospital nurses found that psychological safety, safety climate, and inclusive leadership affected willingness to report errors; it also frames error reporting as a way organizations learn, not merely as personal confession ([Munn et al., 2023](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10599306/)). Because that research concerns nursing teams rather than your workplace, it offers a lens—not proof of why you stayed quiet.

